<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saeima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saeima is the single-chamber parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, made up of 100 members elected by proportional representation. First convened in 1922 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-latvia">Satversme</a>, it was suspended after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>'s coup of 1934 and restored when the fifth Saeima met in July 1993. It elects the president of Latvia and approves the government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saeima.jpg" alt="Saeima" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Great Brightstar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saeima_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Saeima traces its origins to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, which led to the Sejm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and later that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Polish Livonia, part of the Commonwealth, was introduced to the Sejm system. The Warsaw Sejm of 1677 settled the status of what remained of it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a>, naming it a voivodeship and a duchy with the right to name three senators, and it sent six deputies to the Sejm. The rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> belonged to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia, governed by its dukes and the Landtag of Courland.</p><p>The word sejm comes from a Slavic verb meaning to get together. In the 19th century Juris Alunāns of the Young Latvians claimed saeims as a purely Latvian word that he had invented, from the archaic eima, meaning to go. He could not explain the added s- prefix.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In pre-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> the Saeima was elected for three-year terms. The first met from 7 November 1922 to 2 November 1925, the second from 3 November 1925 to 5 November 1928, the third from 6 November 1928 to 2 November 1931, and the fourth from 3 November 1931 until the Latvian coup d'état of 15 May 1934. Its rules of procedure were adopted on 23 March 1923 and amended in 1929 and 1994. Today the term is four years. On 28 May 2011 President Valdis Zatlers initiated the dissolution of the Saeima, which a referendum approved, and it was dissolved on 23 July 2011.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Saeima is unicameral, with 100 members elected by proportional representation from five constituencies: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Kurzeme</a> (11 deputies), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> (12), Riga (38), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> (26) and Zemgale (13). Overseas votes count for Riga. Seats go to parties that win at least 5% of the national vote, by the Webster/Sainte-Laguë method. Voters may mark a candidate FOR or AGAINST, so that a candidate's total is the list's votes plus positive votes minus negative ones. No party has usually won more than 30%, so coalitions have always been necessary.</p><p>All citizens over 18 may vote, and candidates must be over 21. Former employees of the USSR and Latvian SSR security and intelligence services are barred. If the President proposes dissolution, a national referendum must confirm it, and if it fails the President is removed from office. A petition of one-tenth of the electorate can also force a referendum. The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> is the only banned party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saeima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The single-chamber <b>Saeima</b>, elected by proportional representation, chose the president, and Jānis Čakste served as the first president from 1922 until his death in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
